//? Title: Dense Linear Algebra: Factorizations, Conditioning and Least Squares
//? Subtitle: LU, Cholesky, Householder QR and the singular value decomposition in datamunge::linalg, compared on the Motor Trend car data
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Almost every statistical fit ends in a dense linear algebra problem, and the way that problem
is solved decides how many correct digits survive. This report surveys the dense toolkit in
`dm::linalg`: the `DenseMatrix` type, the LU, Cholesky and Householder QR factorizations, and a
new one-sided Jacobi singular value decomposition. Each method is derived, and their costs and
error bounds are stated. The running example is least squares on `dm::datasets::mtcars()`:
fuel consumption regressed on the other ten measurements, then on a polynomial in weight whose
condition number grows from \(10^1\) to \(10^{15}\). The central result is that the normal
equations square the condition number, \(\kappa(X^\top X)=\kappa(X)^2\). We prove this and then
watch it happen. At degree 8 the normal equations keep about one correct digit, while QR keeps
nine, and both errors match the predictions \(u\kappa^2\) and \(u\kappa\) once the harmless
part of \(\kappa\), column scaling, is removed. The SVD then handles an exactly collinear design
that defeats the other two methods. A timing study confirms the cubic cost of every
factorization. Building the report fixed five library bugs. `stats::LM`'s standard errors came
from \(X^\top X\) and were 88% wrong at \(\kappa=10^9\). The Jacobi eigensolver's absolute
stopping test returned wrong eigenvalues for small-scale matrices. QR's rank test was not scale
invariant. Formulas such as `mpg ~ . - model` ignored the `- model`. And `RPlot::text()` colours
were overridden by the SVG's stylesheet.
)

\toc

> The data

`dm::datasets::mtcars()` is R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* road tests,
with fuel consumption `mpg` (miles per US gallon) and ten design and performance variables.
It is small, but it is a famously awkward regression problem, because the predictors measure
overlapping things: engine displacement `disp`, cylinder count `cyl`, horsepower `hp` and weight
`wt` all describe how big the car is.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  std::print("{} rows, {} columns\n\n{}\n\n", cars.nrows(), cars.ncols(), cars.head(5).to_md());
  std::print("{}\n", cars
                         | dm::pivot_longer({"mpg", "cyl", "disp", "hp", "drat", "wt", "qsec", "vs",
                                             "am", "gear", "carb"},
                                            "variable", "value")
                         | dm::group_by("variable")
                         | dm::summarise(agg::mean("value", "Mean"), agg::sd("value", "SD"),
                                         agg::min("value", "Min"), agg::max("value", "Max"))
                         | dm::to_md(2));
}
```
