\caption(The first five cars, and a summary of every numeric column. The standard deviations range from 0.5 (the 0/1 indicators `vs` and `am`) to 124 (`disp`, in cubic inches).)

The scales differ by more than two orders of magnitude, and the correlations are strong. The
next program builds the design matrix through the formula interface used by `stats::LM`
(`mpg ~ . - model` keeps every numeric column) and draws the correlation matrix of the ten
predictors, \(R=Z^\top Z/(n-1)\) with \(Z\) the standardized columns.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dense_linear_algebra_correlation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto cars   = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto design = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model - 1").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true);
  const auto names  = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model - 1").resolve(cars).coefficient_names;
  const auto R      = dm::linalg::correlation_matrix(design.X);
  const std::size_t p = R.rows();

  std::vector<double> pos(p);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(p, std::vector<double>(p));
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < p; ++i) {
    pos[i] = static_cast<double>(i);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < p; ++j) z[i][j] = R(i, p - 1 - j);  // first variable on top
  }
  std::vector<std::string> reversed(names.rbegin(), names.rend());
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image(pos, pos, z, dm::graphics::diverging_colors(21), -1, 1);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < p; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < p; ++j)
      plot.text(pos[i], pos[j], std::format("{:.2f}", z[i][j]),
                std::abs(z[i][j]) > 0.7 ? dm::RGB{255, 255, 255} : dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 11);
  plot.x_ticks(pos, names).y_ticks(pos, reversed).show_grid(false).x_label("").y_label("")
      .title("Correlations between the ten mtcars predictors")
      .size(720, 660)
      .save_svg("build/dense_linear_algebra_correlation.svg");

  double largest = 0;
  std::string pair;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < p; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = i + 1; j < p; ++j)
      if (std::abs(R(i, j)) > largest) largest = std::abs(R(i, j)), pair = names[i] + " and " + names[j];
  std::print("Largest |correlation|: {:.3f} ({}).\n", largest, pair);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Largest |correlation|: 0.902 (cyl and disp).
\image(figures/dense_linear_algebra_correlation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Correlation matrix of the ten predictors, blue for negative and red for positive. The size variables `cyl`, `disp`, `hp` and `wt` form one strongly correlated block.)
\alttext(A ten by ten heat map of correlations. A red block of values between 0.66 and 0.90 joins cyl, disp, hp and wt, and these are negatively correlated, in blue, with drat and vs, with values between -0.45 and -0.81.)

Strong correlations make the columns of \(X\) nearly linearly dependent, and that is what
the condition number measures. The rest of the report is about solving
\(\min_\beta\lVert X\beta-y\rVert_2\) for such an \(X\).

> Dense matrices and their norms

A `dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<T>` stores its \(m\times n\) elements row-major in one contiguous
vector, so element \((i,j)\) sits at offset \(in+j\). The product \(C=AB\) is computed with an
\(i\)-\(k\)-\(j\) loop. The innermost loop then runs along a row of \(B\) and a row of \(C\), both
contiguous in memory, so a product of an \(m\times k\) and a \(k\times n\) matrix costs \(2mkn\)
floating-point operations (*flops*) at close to the machine's streaming speed. The class has the
three induced norms \(\lVert A\rVert_1\) (largest column sum), \(\lVert A\rVert_\infty\) (largest
row sum) and, through the SVD, \(\lVert A\rVert_2=\sigma_1\), plus the Frobenius norm
\(\lVert A\rVert_F=(\sum a_{ij}^2)^{1/2}\). Writing \(A=U\Sigma V^\top\) gives
\(\lVert A\rVert_F^2=\sum_i\sigma_i^2\), and hence for a matrix of rank \(r\)

$$
\lVert A\rVert_2\le\lVert A\rVert_F\le\sqrt r\,\lVert A\rVert_2,
\qquad
\lVert A\rVert_2\le\sqrt{\lVert A\rVert_1\lVert A\rVert_\infty}.
$$
\alttext(The 2-norm of A is at most its Frobenius norm, which is at most root r times the 2-norm. The 2-norm is also at most the square root of the 1-norm times the infinity-norm.)

The second inequality holds because \(\sigma_1^2\) is the largest eigenvalue of \(A^\top A\),
and every eigenvalue is bounded by any induced norm:
\(\sigma_1^2\le\lVert A^\top A\rVert_1\le\lVert A^\top\rVert_1\lVert A\rVert_1=\lVert A\rVert_\infty\lVert A\rVert_1\).
On the mtcars design (intercept plus ten predictors) these bounds are far from tight, because
`disp` and `hp` dominate every norm:

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto X    = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true).X;
  const auto f    = dm::linalg::svd(X);
  std::print("X is {} x {}, rank {}\n\n", X.rows(), X.cols(), f.rank());
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| --- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| \\(\\lVert X\\rVert_2=\\sigma_1\\) | {:.2f} |\n", f.norm_2());
  std::print("| \\(\\lVert X\\rVert_F\\) | {:.2f} |\n", X.norm_frobenius());
  std::print("| \\(\\sqrt{{11}}\\,\\lVert X\\rVert_2\\) | {:.2f} |\n", std::sqrt(11.0) * f.norm_2());
  std::print("| \\(\\lVert X\\rVert_1\\) | {:.2f} |\n", X.norm_1());
  std::print("| \\(\\lVert X\\rVert_\\infty\\) | {:.2f} |\n", X.norm_inf());
  std::print("| \\(\\sqrt{{\\lVert X\\rVert_1\\lVert X\\rVert_\\infty}}\\) | {:.2f} |\n",
             std::sqrt(X.norm_1() * X.norm_inf()));
  std::print("| \\(\\sigma_{{11}}\\) | {:.4f} |\n", f.s.back());
  std::print("| \\(\\kappa_2(X)=\\sigma_1/\\sigma_{{11}}\\) | {:.0f} |\n", f.condition_number());
}
```
