> The least-squares problem

Given \(X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}\) with \(n\ge p\) and \(y\in\mathbb R^n\), find \(\beta\)
minimizing \(f(\beta)=\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2\). Expanding,
\(f(\beta)=y^\top y-2\beta^\top X^\top y+\beta^\top X^\top X\beta\), a quadratic with gradient
\(2(X^\top X\beta-X^\top y)\) and constant Hessian \(2X^\top X\), which is positive semidefinite
because \(v^\top X^\top Xv=\lVert Xv\rVert^2\ge0\). So the minimizers are exactly the solutions
of the *normal equations*

$$
X^\top X\,\hat\beta=X^\top y
\iff X^\top(y-X\hat\beta)=0 .
$$
\alttext(X transpose X times beta hat equals X transpose y, which is equivalent to X transpose times the residual y minus X beta hat being zero.)

The second form is the geometry. The residual \(r=y-X\hat\beta\) is orthogonal to every column
of \(X\), so \(X\hat\beta\) is the orthogonal projection of \(y\) onto the column space. The
minimizer is unique exactly when \(X^\top X\) is invertible, that is, when \(Xv=0\) only for
\(v=0\) (full column rank). Three algorithms solve this problem, and they differ in what they
do with the matrix.

>> Normal equations and the Cholesky factorization

The direct route forms the \(p\times p\) Gram matrix \(G=X^\top X\) (`dm::linalg::gram`,
\(np^2\) flops, using symmetry) and solves \(G\hat\beta=X^\top y\). \(G\) is symmetric positive
definite (SPD) when \(X\) has full rank, and SPD matrices have a *Cholesky factorization*
\(G=LL^\top\) with \(L\) lower triangular and \(\ell_{ii}>0\).

*Existence, by induction on \(p\).* Partition
\(G=\begin{pmatrix}g_{11}&g^\top\\ g&G_{22}\end{pmatrix}\). Positive definiteness gives
\(g_{11}=e_1^\top Ge_1>0\), so set \(\ell_{11}=\sqrt{g_{11}}\) and \(\ell=g/\ell_{11}\). Then

$$
G=\begin{pmatrix}\ell_{11}&0\\ \ell&I\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\ 0&S\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\ell_{11}&\ell^\top\\ 0&I\end{pmatrix},
\qquad S=G_{22}-\frac{gg^\top}{g_{11}} .
$$
\alttext(G equals the block matrix with l 1 1, zero, l and I, times the block diagonal matrix with 1 and S, times the transpose of the first factor. S, the Schur complement, equals G 2 2 minus g g transpose over g 1 1.)

The Schur complement \(S\) is SPD: for any \(w\ne0\) take
\(v=(-g^\top w/g_{11},\,w)\), and then \(w^\top Sw=v^\top Gv>0\). By induction \(S=L_2L_2^\top\), which
completes \(L\). Each pivot \(\ell_{ii}^2\) is a Schur complement's corner entry, so the
factorization fails (a pivot \(\le0\)) exactly when \(G\) is not positive definite. `cholesky()`
therefore doubles as a definiteness test, and its `ok` flag reports the outcome. The algorithm
costs \(p^3/3\) flops and needs no pivoting: \(\ell_{ij}^2\le\sum_k\ell_{ik}^2=g_{ii}\), so no entry
can grow. Two triangular solves \(Lz=X^\top y\), \(L^\top\hat\beta=z\) finish the job. In total
the normal equations cost about \(np^2+p^3/3\) flops, the cheapest of the three methods.

>> Householder QR

The orthogonal route never forms \(G\). Orthogonal matrices preserve lengths, so for any
orthogonal \(Q\), \(\lVert y-X\beta\rVert=\lVert Q^\top y-Q^\top X\beta\rVert\). If \(X=QR\)
with \(R=\begin{pmatrix}R_1\\0\end{pmatrix}\), \(R_1\) upper triangular, and
\(Q^\top y=\begin{pmatrix}c_1\\c_2\end{pmatrix}\), then

$$
\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2=\lVert c_1-R_1\beta\rVert_2^2+\lVert c_2\rVert_2^2 ,
$$
\alttext(The squared norm of y minus X beta equals the squared norm of c 1 minus R 1 beta plus the squared norm of c 2.)

and the minimizer solves the triangular system \(R_1\hat\beta=c_1\), with residual norm
\(\lVert c_2\rVert\). The factorization is built from *Householder reflections*
\(H=I-2vv^\top\) with \(\lVert v\rVert=1\): \(H\) is symmetric and orthogonal
(\(H^2=I-4vv^\top+4v(v^\top v)v^\top=I\)), and it reflects across the hyperplane orthogonal to
\(v\). To map a vector \(x\) onto the first axis, \(Hx=\alpha e_1\), the reflection must
preserve length, so \(\alpha=\pm\lVert x\rVert\), and \(v\) must be parallel to the difference
\(x-\alpha e_1\). The sign is a numerical choice. Taking \(\alpha=-\operatorname{sign}(x_1)\lVert x\rVert\)
gives \(v\propto x+\operatorname{sign}(x_1)\lVert x\rVert e_1\), whose first entry is a sum
of two numbers of the same sign, so nothing cancels even when \(x\) already points nearly along
\(e_1\). Step \(j\) of `qr()` applies such a reflection to rows \(j..n\) of column \(j\), zeroing
it below the diagonal without disturbing the columns already finished:

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dense_linear_algebra_householder.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  // The (wt, qsec) pair of the first car as a 2-vector, reflected onto the first axis.
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const double x1 = cars.double_at("wt", 0), x2 = cars.double_at("qsec", 0) / 8.0;
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> A(2, 1, {x1, x2});
  const auto f = dm::linalg::qr(A);
  const double len = std::hypot(x1, x2), alpha = f.R(0, 0);
  const double v1 = x1 + len, v2 = x2;  // v = x + sign(x1)|x| e1, unnormalized
  const double s = 1.3 / std::hypot(v1, v2), m = 2.6 / std::hypot(v1, v2);  // arrow and mirror lengths

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .segments({0}, {0}, {x1}, {x2}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3)
      .segments({0}, {0}, {alpha}, {0}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3)
      .segments({0}, {0}, {v1 * s}, {v2 * s}, dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 1.5)
      .segments({-v2 * m}, {v1 * m}, {v2 * m}, {-v1 * m}, dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2)
      .segments({x1}, {x2}, {alpha}, {0}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1)
      .text(x1 + 0.1, x2 + 0.12, "x", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 16)
      .text(alpha + 0.9, -0.25, "Hx = -|x| e1", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 14)
      .text(v1 * s + 0.05, v2 * s + 0.15, "v", dm::RGB{71, 85, 105}, 14)
      .text(-1.9, 2.0, "mirror: v^T z = 0", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 14)
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 1)
      .abline_v(0, dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 1)
      .x_limits(-3.6, 3.6).y_limits(-2.8, 2.8)
      .title("A Householder reflection maps x onto the first axis")
      .x_label("first coordinate").y_label("second coordinate")
      .size(700, 560)
      .save_svg("build/dense_linear_algebra_householder.svg");
  std::print("x = ({:.4f}, {:.4f}), |x| = {:.4f}, R(0,0) from qr() = {:.4f}\n", x1, x2, len, alpha);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
x = (2.6200, 2.0575), |x| = 3.3313, R(0,0) from qr() = -3.3313
\image(figures/dense_linear_algebra_householder.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The reflection used by one Householder step, on the Mazda RX4's weight and quarter-mile time (divided by 8 to keep the picture square). The mirror, in green, is orthogonal to \(v=x+\lVert x\rVert e_1\). It sends \(x\), in blue, to \(-\lVert x\rVert e_1\), in red, and `qr()` reports that same value as \(R_{11}\).)
\alttext(A plane with a blue vector x in the first quadrant, a gray vector v between x and the positive first axis, a green mirror line through the origin perpendicular to v, and a red vector of the same length as x lying along the negative first axis.)

Computing \(R\) costs \(2np^2-2p^3/3\) flops, about twice the normal equations when
\(n\gg p\). (`qr()` also accumulates the full \(n\times n\) matrix \(Q\), another \(4n^2p\) flops.
That is negligible at \(n=32\) but matters for tall data.) Every reflection is applied exactly
in orthogonal form, so errors are not amplified. The computed \(\hat R\) is the exact factor of
\(X+\Delta X\) with \(\lVert\Delta X\rVert\le c\,np\,u\lVert X\rVert\), where
\(u=2^{-53}\approx1.1\times10^{-16}\) is the unit roundoff. This is *backward stability*.

>> The singular value decomposition

Every \(X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}\) can be written \(X=U\Sigma V^\top\) with orthonormal columns
\(U\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}\), orthogonal \(V\in\mathbb R^{p\times p}\), and
\(\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1\ge\dots\ge\sigma_p\ge0)\). (Take \(V\) and
\(\sigma_i^2\) from the eigendecomposition of the symmetric matrix \(X^\top X\), and set
\(u_i=Xv_i/\sigma_i\). These are orthonormal because \(u_i^\top u_j=v_i^\top X^\top Xv_j/(\sigma_i\sigma_j)=\delta_{ij}\).)
Substituting \(\beta=Vw\), \(U^\top y=c\):

$$
\lVert y-X\beta\rVert^2=\sum_{i\le r}(c_i-\sigma_iw_i)^2+\text{const},
\qquad
\hat\beta=\sum_{i\le r}\frac{u_i^\top y}{\sigma_i}\,v_i=X^{+}y .
$$
\alttext(The squared residual norm equals the sum over i up to r of c i minus sigma i w i, squared, plus a constant. So beta hat equals the sum over i up to r of u i transpose y over sigma i times v i, which is X plus times y.)

When the rank \(r<p\), the coordinates \(w_{r+1},\dots,w_p\) do not affect the residual, and
setting them to zero gives the minimizer of smallest norm. That is the Moore-Penrose
pseudo-inverse solution. Numerically, singular values below \(\max(n,p)\,\varepsilon\,\sigma_1\), with
\(\varepsilon=2u=2^{-52}\) the machine epsilon, are treated as zero (the LAPACK and NumPy convention; `SVDDecomposition::rank()` and `solve()` take
another cut-off as an argument).

The new `dm::linalg::svd()` uses *one-sided Jacobi* (Hestenes, 1958). It applies plane
rotations to pairs of columns of \(X\) until all columns are mutually orthogonal. Then
\(XV=W\) with orthogonal columns \(w_j\), so \(\sigma_j=\lVert w_j\rVert\) and
\(u_j=w_j/\sigma_j\). For columns \(a_p,a_q\) with \(\alpha=\lVert a_p\rVert^2\),
\(\beta=\lVert a_q\rVert^2\) and \(\gamma=a_p^\top a_q\), the rotated pair
\(a_p'=ca_p-sa_q\), \(a_q'=sa_p+ca_q\) is orthogonal when

$$
a_p'^\top a_q'=(c^2-s^2)\gamma+cs(\alpha-\beta)=0
\iff t^2+2\zeta t-1=0,\quad t=\frac sc,\quad \zeta=\frac{\beta-\alpha}{2\gamma} .
$$
\alttext(a p prime transpose a q prime equals c squared minus s squared times gamma, plus c s times alpha minus beta, which is zero. This is equivalent to t squared plus 2 zeta t minus 1 equals 0, with t equal to s over c and zeta equal to beta minus alpha over 2 gamma.)

The smaller root \(t=\operatorname{sign}(\zeta)/(|\zeta|+\sqrt{1+\zeta^2})\), so
\(|t|\le1\) and the rotation angle is at most \(\pi/4\), is the stable choice. Then
\(c=1/\sqrt{1+t^2}\) and \(s=ct\). Each rotation is one step of the classical two-sided Jacobi
method applied to \(X^\top X\) without forming it. It zeroes the \((p,q)\) entry and lowers the
off-diagonal mass \(\sum_{i\ne j}(a_i^\top a_j)^2\) by \(2\gamma^2\), while the total
\(\lVert X^\top X\rVert_F\) stays fixed, so cyclic sweeps converge, and the convergence is
quadratic in the end. A sweep costs about \(3np^2\) flops (plus \(1.5p^3\) to accumulate \(V\)), and
about 6 to 15 sweeps are typical (13 for the \(400\times400\) random matrix timed below). That is slower than bidiagonalization methods. In exchange the
algorithm is simple, backward stable, and accurate in the *relative* sense for small singular
values (Demmel and Veselić, 1992), which makes it a good reference solver.

>> Conditioning: why \(X^\top X\) is the wrong matrix

The 2-norm condition number of a full-rank \(X\) is \(\kappa(X)=\sigma_1/\sigma_p\). From the
SVD, \(X^\top X=V\Sigma^2V^\top\), which is itself an eigendecomposition, so the singular values
of \(X^\top X\) are \(\sigma_i^2\) and

$$
\kappa(X^\top X)=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{\sigma_p^2}=\kappa(X)^2 .
$$
\alttext(The condition number of X transpose X equals sigma 1 squared over sigma p squared, which is the condition number of X, squared.)

For the least-squares problem, perturbation theory (Wedin, 1973) says that if \(X\) and \(y\)
are perturbed by relative amounts \(\epsilon\), the solution changes by

$$
\frac{\lVert\delta\hat\beta\rVert}{\lVert\hat\beta\rVert}
\lesssim\epsilon\left(\kappa+\kappa^2\tan\theta\right),
\qquad \tan\theta=\frac{\lVert r\rVert}{\lVert X\hat\beta\rVert},
$$
\alttext(The relative change in beta hat is at most about epsilon times kappa plus kappa squared times tan theta, where tan theta is the norm of the residual over the norm of the fitted values.)

where \(\theta\) is the angle between \(y\) and the column space. A backward-stable method
(QR, SVD) commits errors equivalent to \(\epsilon\approx u\), so its error obeys this bound: of
order \(u\kappa\) for a nearly consistent system, with a \(\kappa^2\) term weighted by the
residual. The normal equations instead hand Cholesky the matrix \(X^\top X\). Merely rounding
its entries is a relative perturbation of size \(u\) to a matrix with condition number
\(\kappa^2\), so the error is of order \(u\kappa^2\) *whatever the residual*. Once
\(\kappa\gtrsim u^{-1/2}\approx10^8\), the normal equations can return no correct digits at all.

One refinement matters in practice. All three methods are unaffected by scaling the columns of
\(X\). Scaling column \(j\) by \(d_j\) only rescales \(\hat\beta_j\), and the rounding errors scale
with it. So the \(\kappa\) that governs accuracy is that of the column-equilibrated matrix
\(XD^{-1}\), with \(D\) the column norms. By a theorem of van der Sluis, this is within a
factor \(\sqrt p\) of the best \(\kappa\) achievable by any column scaling
(`dm::linalg::equilibrate_columns`). A design whose \(\kappa\) comes only from mixing units,
cubic inches beside thousands of pounds, is not ill-conditioned in any sense that matters.

> Applying it to mtcars

>> The full regression

The first fit regresses `mpg` on all ten predictors plus an intercept. `least_squares()` runs
each method, and `stats::LM` (R's `lm`, which now also uses QR) gives the reference
coefficients.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

int main() {
  const auto cars   = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto design = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true);
  const dm::stats::LM lm(dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model"), cars);
  const auto ne  = la::least_squares(design.X, design.y, la::LeastSquaresMethod::NormalEquations);
  const auto qr  = la::least_squares(design.X, design.y, la::LeastSquaresMethod::HouseholderQR);
  const auto svd = la::least_squares(design.X, design.y, la::LeastSquaresMethod::SVD);
  const auto acc = la::least_squares_accuracy(design.X, design.y);

  std::print("| coefficient | `stats::LM` | Householder QR | normal equations | SVD |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < qr.coefficients.size(); ++j)
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", lm.coefficient_names()[j],
               lm.coefficients()[j], qr.coefficients[j], ne.coefficients[j], svd.coefficients[j]);
  std::print("\n| | normal equations | Householder QR | SVD |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| relative error against a `long double` SVD | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} |\n",
             acc.normal_equations_error, acc.qr_error, acc.svd_error);
  std::print("| residual norm | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n\n", ne.residual_norm, qr.residual_norm,
             svd.residual_norm);
  std::print("kappa(X) = {:.0f}, kappa after equilibrating columns = {:.0f}, tan(theta) = {:.3f}, "
             "u kappa = {:.1e}, u kappa^2 = {:.1e} (equilibrated)\n",
             acc.condition_number, acc.equilibrated_condition_number, acc.tan_theta,
             0x1p-53 * acc.equilibrated_condition_number,
             0x1p-53 * acc.equilibrated_condition_number * acc.equilibrated_condition_number);
}
```
