kappa(X) = 12213, kappa after equilibrating columns = 143, tan(theta) = 0.103, u kappa = 1.6e-14, u kappa^2 = 2.3e-12 (equilibrated)
// result_end
\caption(Coefficients of `mpg ~ .` from four solvers, and each double-precision method's relative error against the same problem solved in `long double`, with unit roundoff \(5\cdot10^{-20}\).)

All four agree to the printed six decimals, because the equilibrated condition number,
143, is modest (the raw \(\kappa\) is 12,213). The errors are of the predicted orders: about \(u\kappa\) for QR and the SVD, and
about \(u\kappa^2\) for the normal equations. With \(\tan\theta\approx0.1\) (the model explains
most of `mpg` but not all of it) the QR error stays near the \(u\kappa\) end of the Wedin bound.
Note what the table does *not* show: none of these coefficients is individually trustworthy as a
statistical estimate (in R's `summary(lm(mpg ~ ., mtcars))` none reaches significance at the 5%
level), but that is a question of the data's information, not of arithmetic.

>> Where the conditioning comes from

The singular values show what makes this design ill-conditioned. The next program compares
the raw design, the column-equilibrated design, and the standardized predictors \(Z\) (centred
and scaled, without the intercept). It also cross-checks the identity
\(\sigma_i(Z)^2/(n-1)=\lambda_i(R)\) against the Jacobi eigensolver applied to the correlation
matrix, which is the link between the SVD and principal components.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dense_linear_algebra_spectrum.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto X    = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true).X;
  const auto P    = dm::formula("mpg ~ . - model - 1").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true).X;
  const auto raw  = la::svd(X);
  const auto eq   = la::svd(la::equilibrate_columns(X));
  const auto Z    = la::svd(la::standardize_columns(P));
  const auto eig  = la::jacobi_eigen(la::correlation_matrix(P));

  std::print("| \\(i\\) | \\(\\sigma_i(X)\\) | \\(\\sigma_i(XD^{{-1}})\\) | \\(\\sigma_i(Z)\\) | "
             "\\(\\sigma_i(Z)^2/31\\) | \\(\\lambda_i(R)\\) (Jacobi) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 11; ++i) {
    if (i < 10)
      std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", i + 1, raw.s[i], eq.s[i], Z.s[i],
                 Z.s[i] * Z.s[i] / 31, eig.eigenvalues[i]);
    else
      std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | | | |\n", i + 1, raw.s[i], eq.s[i]);
  }
  std::print("\n| matrix | \\(\\kappa_2\\) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| \\(X\\) (raw, with intercept) | {:.0f} |\n"
             "| \\(XD^{{-1}}\\) (unit columns) | {:.1f} |\n| \\(Z\\) (standardized predictors) | {:.1f} |\n",
             raw.condition_number(), eq.condition_number(), Z.condition_number());

  std::vector<double> idx, lr, le, lz;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 11; ++i) {
    idx.push_back(static_cast<double>(i + 1));
    lr.push_back(std::log10(raw.s[i] / raw.s[0]));
    le.push_back(std::log10(eq.s[i] / eq.s[0]));
  }
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 10; ++i) lz.push_back(std::log10(Z.s[i] / Z.s[0]));
  std::vector<double> idx10(idx.begin(), idx.end() - 1);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(idx, lr, "raw X", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).points(idx, lr, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6)
      .line(idx, le, "unit columns", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2).points(idx, le, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6)
      .line(idx10, lz, "standardized Z", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2).points(idx10, lz, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 6)
      .y_ticks({0, -1, -2, -3, -4}, {"1", "0.1", "0.01", "0.001", "0.0001"})
      .y_limits(-4.3, 0.8)
      .title("Singular values relative to the largest")
      .x_label("index i").y_label("sigma_i / sigma_1 (log scale)")
      .size(800, 520)
      .save_svg("build/dense_linear_algebra_spectrum.svg");
}
```
