\image(figures/dense_linear_algebra_spectrum.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Singular values of three versions of the mtcars design, and the eigenvalues of the predictors' correlation matrix from `jacobi_eigen`, which match \(\sigma_i(Z)^2/(n-1)\).)
\alttext(Three decreasing curves of relative singular value on a log scale against index 1 to 11. The raw design falls to about 0.0001, the unit-column design to about 0.007, and the standardized predictors only to about 0.06.)

Most of the raw \(\kappa\) is units: equilibrating the columns removes a factor of 85, from
12,213 to 143. Most of what remains is the intercept. The predictors have large means compared
with their spread, so the constant column is nearly a combination of them, and centring removes
that near-dependence. The standardized predictors keep a \(\kappa\) of 15.6, which is the real
collinearity, the red block in the correlation figure. The last two columns of the table agree to six decimals, so
the SVD of the standardized data and the eigendecomposition of its correlation matrix give the
same principal components.

>> A conditioning ladder: polynomials in weight

To see the error laws over many orders of magnitude, fit `mpg` by a polynomial in weight with
raw monomials, \(X_d=[1,\ w,\ w^2,\dots,w^d]\) (`dm::linalg::vandermonde`). Weights lie
between 1.5 and 5.4, so the columns \(w^j\) grow ever more alike as \(d\) increases. For each
degree, `least_squares_accuracy()` solves the problem by all three methods and compares each
answer with a `long double` SVD of the identical data, so the errors are the algorithms' alone.
It does this for the real response, and for a consistent response (the reference fitted values,
residual zero) that removes the \(\kappa^2\tan\theta\) term. The figure plots the
errors against the equilibrated condition number, with the two predicted lines \(u\kappa\)
and \(u\kappa^2\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dense_linear_algebra_ladder.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto wt   = cars.get_column("wt").values();
  const auto mpg  = cars.get_column("mpg").values();
  const double lu = std::log10(0x1p-53);
  std::vector<double> kn, en, kq, eq, kq0, eq0, ks, es;  // log10 kappa_eq and log10 error per method
  auto keep = [](std::vector<double>& k, std::vector<double>& e, double kappa, double err) {
    if (std::isfinite(err) && err > 0) k.push_back(std::log10(kappa)), e.push_back(std::log10(err));
  };

  std::print("| \\(d\\) | \\(\\kappa(X_d)\\) | \\(\\kappa(X_dD^{{-1}})\\) | \\(\\tan\\theta\\) | normal eq. | QR | SVD "
             "| normal eq., consistent | QR, consistent |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t d = 1; d <= 12; ++d) {
    const auto X = la::vandermonde(wt, d);
    const auto a = la::least_squares_accuracy(X, mpg);
    const auto c = la::least_squares_accuracy(X, X * a.reference);  // same X, zero residual
    std::print("| {} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.3f} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} |\n", d,
               a.condition_number, a.equilibrated_condition_number, a.tan_theta, a.normal_equations_error,
               a.qr_error, a.svd_error, c.normal_equations_error, c.qr_error);
    const double k = a.equilibrated_condition_number;
    keep(kn, en, k, a.normal_equations_error), keep(kq, eq, k, a.qr_error);
    keep(kq0, eq0, k, c.qr_error), keep(ks, es, k, a.svd_error);
  }
  std::print("\nu = {:.2e}; nan = the method gave up (Cholesky met a non-positive pivot)\n", 0x1p-53);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .segments({0.5, 0.5}, {lu + 0.5, lu + 1.0}, {11.8, 10.4}, {lu + 11.8, lu + 20.8},
                dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)  // u kappa and u kappa^2, kept inside the axes
      .text(10.6, -3.6, "u kappa", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 13)
      .text(4.6, -4.6, "u kappa^2", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 13)
      .line(kn, en, "normal equations", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).points(kn, en, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 7)
      .line(kq, eq, "Householder QR", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2).points(kq, eq, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 7)
      .points(kq0, eq0, "QR, consistent y", dm::RGB{125, 211, 252}, 6)
      .points(ks, es, "SVD", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 5)
      .x_limits(0.5, 11.8).y_limits(-16.5, 5.5)
      .x_ticks({1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11}, {"10", "1e3", "1e5", "1e7", "1e9", "1e11"})
      .y_ticks({-16, -12, -8, -4, 0}, {"1e-16", "1e-12", "1e-8", "1e-4", "1"})
      .title("Least-squares error against conditioning (mtcars, mpg ~ poly(wt, d))")
      .x_label("kappa of the column-equilibrated design")
      .y_label("relative error of the coefficients")
      .size(860, 600)
      .save_svg("build/dense_linear_algebra_ladder.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(d\) | \(\kappa(X_d)\) | \(\kappa(X_dD^{-1})\) | \(\tan\theta\) | normal eq. | QR | SVD | normal eq., consistent | QR, consistent |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1.3e+01 | 6.8e+00 | 0.142 | 9.1e-15 | 7.5e-16 | 3.7e-15 | 8.3e-15 | 7.5e-16 |
| 2 | 1.4e+02 | 3.8e+01 | 0.121 | 1.9e-13 | 8.3e-16 | 4.1e-16 | 1.7e-13 | 7.0e-16 |
| 3 | 2.8e+03 | 3.5e+02 | 0.121 | 6.0e-12 | 8.5e-15 | 3.8e-15 | 4.6e-12 | 2.0e-15 |
| 4 | 5.9e+04 | 3.2e+03 | 0.120 | 2.0e-10 | 2.8e-13 | 3.8e-13 | 1.9e-10 | 2.8e-14 |
| 5 | 1.1e+06 | 2.4e+04 | 0.119 | 5.5e-09 | 3.6e-13 | 1.5e-12 | 5.6e-09 | 4.8e-13 |
| 6 | 2.3e+07 | 1.9e+05 | 0.117 | 1.3e-07 | 3.2e-13 | 1.1e-11 | 1.3e-07 | 2.6e-12 |
| 7 | 6.3e+08 | 2.1e+06 | 0.115 | 1.1e-04 | 6.8e-11 | 7.9e-11 | 1.1e-04 | 2.6e-11 |
| 8 | 1.6e+10 | 2.2e+07 | 0.114 | 4.9e-02 | 2.1e-10 | 2.3e-09 | 4.9e-02 | 2.1e-10 |
| 9 | 3.2e+11 | 1.7e+08 | 0.105 | 7.1e-01 | 8.9e-10 | 5.0e-10 | 7.1e-01 | 7.3e-10 |
| 10 | 6.6e+12 | 1.4e+09 | 0.103 | 1.7e+00 | 7.1e-08 | 2.6e-09 | 1.7e+00 | 2.9e-08 |
| 11 | 1.9e+14 | 1.7e+10 | 0.100 | nan | 1.9e-07 | 1.0e+00 | nan | 1.3e-07 |
| 12 | 7.3e+15 | 2.7e+11 | 0.098 | nan | 1.3e-06 | 1.0e+00 | nan | 1.4e-06 |

u = 1.11e-16; nan = the method gave up (Cholesky met a non-positive pivot)
\image(figures/dense_linear_algebra_ladder.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Relative coefficient error of each method against a `long double` reference, for polynomial degree \(d=1,\dots,12\). "Consistent" replaces `mpg` by the reference fitted values, so the residual is zero. In the figure each point is one degree, \(d=1\) on the left to \(d=12\) on the right. The normal equations, in red, follow \(u\kappa^2\) and break down at \(d=11\). QR, in blue, and its consistent-response version, in light blue, follow \(u\kappa\). The SVD, in green, follows QR until its rank cut-off discards the smallest singular value at \(d\ge11\).)
\alttext(A log-log plot of error against condition number, with two gray reference lines of slope 1 and 2. Red normal-equation points climb along the slope-2 line from 1e-15 to near 1. Blue QR points and green SVD points stay along or below the slope-1 line, ending near 1e-6. Two green points at the far right sit at error 1.)

Three things stand out.

- The **normal equations** track \(u\kappa^2\) closely. At \(d=8\) the prediction is
  \(u\kappa_{eq}^2\approx0.05\), and the measured error is 0.049: about one correct digit. From
  \(d=11\), \(\kappa_{eq}^2\) exceeds \(1/u\). The rounded \(X^\top X\) is then no longer
  positive definite, Cholesky meets a negative pivot, and the method gives up.
- **QR** stays at or below \(u\kappa\) all the way, about nine correct digits at \(d=8\)
  and six at \(d=12\). The residual term \(u\kappa^2\tan\theta\) of the Wedin bound never
  appears, even though \(\tan\theta\approx0.1\): the real response and the consistent one give
  errors within a factor of ten of each other, usually much closer, with no trend in \(\kappa\). The bound is a worst case over all perturbations of size \(u\), and
  the structured errors of a Householder sweep do not come close to it on these data.
- The **SVD** also stays below \(u\kappa\) until \(d=11\). There, \(\sigma_{\min}/\sigma_1\) of
  the *raw* design falls below the default cut-off \(32\varepsilon\approx7\times10^{-15}\) (the
  cut-off is not scale invariant), the SVD treats the design as rank-deficient,
  and it returns the minimum-norm solution of the truncated problem, a different answer (error
  near 1). Passing a smaller `rcond` to `SVDDecomposition::solve` recovers QR-like accuracy. Whether
  that is wise is a statistical question: the discarded direction is pure noise amplification.

The plot is against the *equilibrated* \(\kappa\). The raw \(\kappa(X_d)\) is larger by up to a
factor of 27,000 (at \(d=12\)), because the column \(w^{12}\) is about \(10^{9}\) times longer
than the constant column. Against it, the measured slopes are much shallower than 1 and 2. That
is the van der Sluis effect at work.

>> Standard errors from `stats::LM`

The coefficients are only half of a regression. The covariance
\(\hat\sigma^2(X^\top X)^{-1}\) gives the standard errors, and until this report
`linalg::linear_regression` (behind `stats::LM`) computed it by Cholesky-inverting
\(X^\top X\), squaring \(\kappa\) again even though the coefficients came from QR. It now uses
the QR factor, \((X^\top X)^{-1}=R_1^{-1}R_1^{-\top}\) (`QRDecomposition::r_inverse`), which
never forms \(X^\top X\). The check below compares `LM`'s standard errors for
`mpg ~ poly(wt, d)` with \(\hat\sigma\lVert\Sigma^{-1}V^\top e_j\rVert\) from the SVD.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  std::print("| \\(d\\) | \\(\\kappa(X_d)\\) | max relative difference of `LM` standard errors from the SVD |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int d : {3, 6, 8, 9}) {
    const auto formula = std::format("mpg ~ poly(wt, {})", d);
    const dm::stats::LM lm(dm::formula(formula), cars);
    const auto X = dm::formula(formula).resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true).X;
    const auto f = la::svd(X);
    double worst = 0;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < X.cols(); ++j) {
      double v = 0;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < X.cols(); ++k) v += std::pow(f.V(j, k) / f.s[k], 2);
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(lm.standard_errors()[j] / (lm.sigma() * std::sqrt(v)) - 1));
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} |\n", d, f.condition_number(), worst);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(d\) | \(\kappa(X_d)\) | max relative difference of `LM` standard errors from the SVD |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 3 | 2.8e+03 | 8.4e-15 |
| 6 | 2.3e+07 | 2.4e-12 |
| 8 | 1.6e+10 | 2.0e-10 |
| 9 | 3.2e+11 | 1.7e-09 |
// result_end
\caption(Agreement between `stats::LM`'s standard errors and the SVD formula. The differences are of order \(u\kappa\). Before the change, the Cholesky route had errors of order \(u\kappa^2\): on synthetic designs, 0.4% at \(\kappa=10^7\) and 88% at \(\kappa=10^9\).)

>> A rank-deficient design

Add a column that measures the same thing in other units: displacement in litres,
\(\texttt{disp}/61.0237\). The design now has an exact linear dependence (up to the rounding of
that one division). Coefficients are no longer identified, so any split of the displacement
effect between the two columns fits equally well.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

int main() {
  auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  std::vector<double> litres;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cars.nrows(); ++i) litres.push_back(cars.double_at("disp", i) / 61.0237);
  cars.add_column("disp_l", litres);
  const auto info   = dm::formula("mpg ~ wt + disp + disp_l").resolve(cars);
  const auto design = info.build_matrix(cars, true);
  const auto f      = la::svd(design.X);
  std::print("singular values:");
  for (double s : f.s) std::print(" {:.3e}", s);
  std::print("\nnumerical rank {} of {}, kappa = {:.1e}\n\n", f.rank(), design.X.cols(), f.condition_number());

  std::print("| method | outcome | `wt` | `disp` | `disp_l` | residual norm |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto m : {la::LeastSquaresMethod::NormalEquations, la::LeastSquaresMethod::HouseholderQR,
                 la::LeastSquaresMethod::SVD}) {
    try {
      const auto r = la::least_squares(design.X, design.y, m);
      std::print("| {} | rank {} | {:.5f} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} | {:.6f} |\n", la::to_string(m), r.rank,
                 r.coefficients[1], r.coefficients[2], r.coefficients[3], r.residual_norm);
    } catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
      std::print("| {} | {} | | | | |\n", la::to_string(m), e.what());
    }
  }
  const auto base = la::least_squares(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt + disp").resolve(cars).build_matrix(cars, true).X,
                                      design.y);
  std::print("| QR without `disp_l` | rank 2 | {:.5f} | {:.7f} | | {:.6f} |\n", base.coefficients[1],
             base.coefficients[2], base.residual_norm);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
singular values: 1.477e+03 5.448e+00 1.216e+00 2.989e-15
numerical rank 3 of 4, kappa = 4.9e+17

| method | outcome | `wt` | `disp` | `disp_l` | residual norm |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| normal equations | least_squares: X^T X is not numerically positive definite | | | | |
| Householder QR | least_squares: X is rank-deficient; use LeastSquaresMethod::SVD | | | | |
| SVD | rank 3 | -3.35083 | -0.0177200 | -0.0002904 | 15.706129 |
| QR without `disp_l` | rank 2 | -3.35083 | -0.0177247 | | 15.706129 |
// result_end
\caption(An exactly collinear design. The minimum-norm SVD solution divides the displacement effect between the two columns in proportion to their scales.)

The rounded quotient leaves \(\sigma_4/\sigma_1=2\times10^{-18}\), below the unit roundoff, so
the problem is singular to working precision, and the three methods respond differently.
Cholesky meets a non-positive pivot, so the normal equations stop. (With a less exact
dependence a tiny positive pivot can slip through instead, and then the method returns large,
meaningless coefficients.) QR detects that \(|R_{44}|\) is below its threshold
\(\max(n,p)\,\varepsilon\max|R_{ii}|\) and refuses. The SVD drops the null direction and returns the
minimum-norm solution. Its residual equals the reduced model's, and its two displacement
coefficients satisfy \(b_\texttt{disp}+b_\texttt{disp\_l}/61.0237=\hat b_\texttt{disp}\)
(the reduced model's effect per cubic inch), split so that the coefficient vector is as short as
possible: \(b_\texttt{disp\_l}/b_\texttt{disp}=1/61.0237\). (QR's threshold used to be an
absolute \(10^{-12}\), so whether a design counted as rank-deficient depended on its units. It is
now relative to the largest \(|R_{ii}|\).)

>> What it costs

Every dense factorization costs \(O(n^3)\) for an \(n\times n\) matrix, but the constants differ:
\(\tfrac13n^3\) flops for Cholesky, \(\tfrac23n^3\) for LU, \(\tfrac43n^3\) for the
Householder \(R\) (plus about \(2n^3\) more for the explicit \(Q\)), \(2n^3\) for a product,
and several sweeps of about \(4.5n^3\) for one-sided Jacobi. The timing below uses random SPD
and general matrices; times are for this machine and vary between runs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dense_linear_algebra_timing.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <random>

namespace la = dm::linalg;

template <class F> double seconds(F&& f) {
  const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  f();
  return std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
}

int main() {
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1974);
  std::normal_distribution<double> z;
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Cholesky", "LU", "product", "QR (with Q)", "SVD (Jacobi)"};
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> cols{{22, 163, 74}, {37, 99, 235}, {100, 116, 139}, {234, 88, 12}, {220, 38, 38}};
  std::vector<double> sizes{50, 100, 200, 400};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> t(names.size());
  std::size_t sweeps = 0;
  double lu_backward = 0;
  for (double nd : sizes) {
    const auto n = static_cast<std::size_t>(nd);
    la::DenseMatrix<double> A(n, n);
    for (auto& v : A.data()) v = z(rng);
    auto S = la::gram(A);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) S(i, i) += static_cast<double>(n);
    t[0].push_back(seconds([&] { (void)la::cholesky(S); }));
    t[1].push_back(seconds([&] { (void)la::lu(A); }));
    t[2].push_back(seconds([&] { (void)(A * A); }));
    t[3].push_back(seconds([&] { (void)la::qr(A); }));
    t[4].push_back(seconds([&] { sweeps = la::svd(A).sweeps; }));
    const std::vector<double> b(n, 1.0);
    lu_backward = la::backward_error(A, la::lu(A).solve(b), b);
  }
  std::print("| method |");
  for (double n : sizes) std::print(" \\(n={}\\) |", n);
  std::print(" log-log slope 200 to 400 |\n| --- |");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i <= sizes.size(); ++i) std::print(" ---: |");
  std::print("\n");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> lx;
  for (double n : sizes) lx.push_back(std::log10(n));
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < names.size(); ++m) {
    std::print("| {} |", names[m]);
    std::vector<double> ly;
    for (double s : t[m]) std::print(" {:.4f} s |", s), ly.push_back(std::log10(s));
    std::print(" {:.2f} |\n", std::log2(t[m][3] / t[m][2]));
    plot.line(lx, ly, names[m], cols[m], 2).points(lx, ly, "", cols[m], 6);
  }
  std::print("\nJacobi sweeps at n = 400: {}; LU backward error at n = 400: {:.1e}\n", sweeps, lu_backward);
  const double c0 = std::log10(t[0][0]) - 0.4;  // slope-3 reference line below Cholesky
  plot.segments({lx.front()}, {c0}, {lx.back()}, {c0 + 3 * (lx.back() - lx.front())}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1)
      .x_ticks(lx, {"50", "100", "200", "400"})
      .y_ticks({-5, -4, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1}, {"1e-5", "1e-4", "0.001", "0.01", "0.1", "1", "10"})
      .y_limits(-5.3, 1.8)
      .title("Time of dense factorizations of an n x n matrix")
      .x_label("n (log scale)").y_label("seconds (log scale)")
      .size(800, 540)
      .save_svg("build/dense_linear_algebra_timing.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | \(n=50\) | \(n=100\) | \(n=200\) | \(n=400\) | log-log slope 200 to 400 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Cholesky | 0.0000 s | 0.0001 s | 0.0007 s | 0.0050 s | 2.93 |
| LU | 0.0000 s | 0.0002 s | 0.0013 s | 0.0102 s | 3.02 |
| product | 0.0000 s | 0.0002 s | 0.0015 s | 0.0127 s | 3.10 |
| QR (with Q) | 0.0001 s | 0.0008 s | 0.0065 s | 0.0547 s | 3.07 |
| SVD (Jacobi) | 0.0010 s | 0.0077 s | 0.0627 s | 0.5452 s | 3.12 |

Jacobi sweeps at n = 400: 13; LU backward error at n = 400: 2.7e-16
\image(figures/dense_linear_algebra_timing.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Wall-clock time of each factorization. The last column is \(\log_2\) of the time ratio between \(n=400\) and \(n=200\), which is 3 for an exactly cubic cost. The gray line has slope 3.)
\alttext(A log-log plot of seconds against n from 50 to 400. Five roughly parallel rising lines, from Cholesky at the bottom through LU, the matrix product and QR to the Jacobi SVD at the top, all with slope close to the gray slope-3 reference line.)

The slopes sit within 0.1 of 3 (they vary a little between runs), as they should; any excess shows the
\(400\times400\) matrices (1.3 MB) starting to spill out of the faster caches. The time ratios
follow the flop counts: Cholesky takes about half as long as LU (predicted \(\tfrac12\)), and QR
with its explicit \(Q\) about five times as long (predicted \((\tfrac43+2)/\tfrac23=5\)). The one
exception is the product, which does three times LU's flops in about the same time, because
its inner loop streams through contiguous rows while elimination's access pattern does not. The
SVD is about a hundred times slower than Cholesky, a fair price for a reference solution, but
it is not a routine solver for large problems. The
LU backward error \(\lVert b-A\hat x\rVert/(\lVert A\rVert\lVert\hat x\rVert+\lVert b\rVert)\),
of order \(u\), is the backward stability of partial pivoting seen directly.

> Conclusion

- **Do not solve least squares by the normal equations unless the design is well conditioned.**
  Forming \(X^\top X\) squares the condition number. On mtcars polynomials the normal equations
  lost digits exactly as \(u\kappa^2\) predicts and failed outright past \(\kappa\approx10^8\).
  Householder QR costs about twice as much and keeps \(u\kappa\). That is why R's `lm`, and now
  every part of `stats::LM` including its standard errors, uses QR.
- **Measure conditioning after equilibrating columns.** The raw \(\kappa\) of a design
  conflates units with collinearity. Scaling is free and harmless for every method, so
  only the equilibrated \(\kappa\) predicts accuracy. Centring then removes the large-mean
  collinearity with the intercept. For mtcars these two steps take \(\kappa\) from 12,213 to 15.6.
- **Use the SVD when rank is in doubt.** It exposes the spectrum, returns a sensible
  minimum-norm answer for a singular design, and doubles as principal components analysis. Its
  cut-off is a modelling choice, as the \(d\ge11\) polynomials showed. It is also the slowest
  method by far.
- **Limits.** These are textbook dense algorithms with plain loops: no blocking, BLAS or
  threads. They suit the \(p\lesssim\) few hundred problems of classical statistics. `qr()` forms
  an explicit \(n\times n\) \(Q\), which wastes \(O(n^2)\) memory on tall data. The Wedin
  bound's \(\kappa^2\tan\theta\) term, invisible here, can appear for adversarial data or large
  residuals, so QR is not immune to squaring either.

The library changes made for this report are the new `linalg/svd.hpp` (`svd`,
`SVDDecomposition`, `condition_number`) and `linalg/least_squares.hpp` (`least_squares`,
`LeastSquaresMethod`, `gram`, `vandermonde`, `matrix_cast`, `equilibrate_columns`,
`standardize_columns`, `correlation_matrix`, `relative_error`, `backward_error`,
`least_squares_accuracy`), `QRDecomposition::r_inverse`/`rank_threshold`, and
`graphics::diverging_colors`. Five bugs were fixed along the way. `linear_regression`'s
covariance now comes from \(R\), not from Cholesky on \(X^\top X\). `jacobi_eigen` used an
absolute stopping test and returned \((10^{-7},10^{-7})\) as the eigenvalues of
\(10^{-7}\begin{psmallmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{psmallmatrix}\), whose true eigenvalues are
\(2\times10^{-7}\) and 0. QR's rank test was absolute, so it depended on units. In a formula,
`. - x` subtracted `x` before `.` was expanded, so `x` stayed in the model; R drops it. And the
SVG writer's stylesheet rule `text{fill:...}` overrode every `RPlot::text()` colour in every
renderer; it now applies only to text without its own colour.
